<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bar, Montenegro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Antivari</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bar is a port town on the Adriatic coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. Its medieval predecessor, Stari Bar, was an important town of Duklja and became the seat of an archbishopric in 1089. Under Venetian and then Ottoman rule from 1571, it passed to Montenegro in 1878. The modern town grew by the sea and is the terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>–Bar railway, completed in 1976.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bar-montenegro.jpg" alt="Bar, Montenegro" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stari_Bar,_Montenegro,_2014-04-18,_DD_03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bar lies on the Adriatic shore in the coastal region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, about 53 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica">Podgorica</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-skadar">Lake Skadar</a>, the largest lake in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, to the east. Its name is thought to be a shortened Slavic form of Antivari, from the Latin Antibarum or Antibari, meaning opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, for its position across the Adriatic from that Italian city. It is called Tivari in Albanian. Finds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> show that people lived there in prehistoric times, and the name Antibarium is first quoted in the 10th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Middle Ages Bar was linked to the Slavic state of Duklja. Stefan Vojislav, ruler from 1018 to 1043, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in a battle on a hill near Bar and made it his seat. His son Mihailo I established the Archdiocese of Antivari, founded in 1089. Byzantium held the town from 1166 to 1183, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a> conquered and destroyed it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a> ruled it until 1360, then the Balšić family. The Venetians controlled Bar from 1405 to 1412, when it grew most and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>, with its own Statute of Communes and coinage. They ruled again from 1443 to 1571, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> captured Antivari and held it until 1878.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ceded Antivari to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> at the Treaty of Berlin in 1878, and the town was made militarily neutral. In the late 1850s it had 4,000 inhabitants, 62.5 per cent of them Muslims; more than half left or were expelled after 1878. On 30 August 1904 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-marconi">Guglielmo Marconi</a> made a radio link between Antivari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, and in 1908 the first railway in this part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> opened there. In 1914 and 1915 Austro-Hungarian ships bombarded the port, and on 16 August 1914 a French fleet sank the cruiser SMS Zenta off the town in the Battle of Antivari. Bar joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> in 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The old town of Stari Bar and the newer settlement by the sea are distinct: the district of Pristan was demolished in 1976 to enlarge the port, and its people moved mostly to Topolica. The Port of Bar and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>–Bar railway, opened in 1976, are the basis of its economy. King Nikola's castle at Topolica, built in 1885, houses the Bar Homeland Museum. Bar is the birthplace of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovan-vladimir">Jovan Vladimir</a>, and has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic churches and mosques built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bar, Montenegro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He entrusted the south to Frederick, count of <b>Bar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1040 Stefan Vojislav threw off Byzantine control and defeated a Byzantine army near <b>Bar</b> in 1042.</blockquote><a href="/state/duklja">Duklja</a></li><li><blockquote>War against the Ottomans in 1876 to 1878 won recognition of independence at Berlin in 1878, nearly doubling the territory and gaining Nikšić, Podgorica, <b>Bar</b> and later Ulcinj.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-montenegro">Principality of Montenegro</a></li></ul></section>
